{"step_index":1222,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-17T03:35:05Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-17T12:35:05+09:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-17T12:35:05+09:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-17T12:35:05+09:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-17T12:35:05+09:00\nShowing lines 1-60 of 130\n1: ---\n2: name: generative_ui\n3: description: How to render rich interactive HTML widgets inline in the chat or as standalone artifacts. Use this skill when you want to show the user diagrams, data visualizations, interactive controls, educational walkthroughs, or any rich visual content beyond plain text and markdown.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # Generative UI\n7: \n8: You can render custom, rich, interactive user interfaces (inline widgets or\n9: larger artifacts) directly in the chat. This is a great way to communicate\n10: complex information to the user, generate rich visualizations, and even create\n11: small interactive experiences for the user.\n12: \n13: ## Workflow\n14: \n15: 1.  **Create the HTML Artifact**: Save a self-contained `.html` file (using\n16:     Tailwind CSS and inline JavaScript) directly in the artifact directory using\n17:     your native file-creation tool (not shell or Python scripts, which skip\n18:     `ArtifactMetadata`), with `UserFacing: true` and `RequestFeedback: false` in\n19:     `ArtifactMetadata`.\n20: 2.  **Embed Inline (optional)**: Include the `<agent-embed>` tag in your chat\n21:     response if the HTML artifact should render inline in the conversation:\n22: \n23:     ```\n24:     <agent-embed src=\"file:///<artifact_path>/widget.html\"></agent-embed>\n25:     ```\n26: \n27: ## Constraints & Theming\n28: \n29: *   **External Assets & Tailwind CSS**: All external CDNs are blocked by CSP,\n30:     except for one allowlisted gstatic Tailwind dependency that you **CAN** and\n31:     **SHOULD** use to style your artifacts. Include the following script tag in\n32:     your `<head>` to enable Tailwind:\n33: \n34:     ```html\n35:     <script src=\"https://www.gstatic.com/antigravity/web/dev/tailwindcss.min.js\"></script>\n36:     ```\n37: \n38: *   **Provided Container Defaults & Theme Variables**: The iframe container\n39:     already sets `margin: 0`, `padding: 0`, a transparent background,\n40:     `--foreground` text, and `14px` body typography, so you do not need to set a\n41:     `font-family`. Semantic tokens are injected for surfaces (`--background`,\n42:     `--content`, `--card`, `--sidebar`), borders (`--card-border`, `--border`),\n43:     text (`--foreground`, `--muted-foreground`, `--placeholder`,\n44:     `--code-foreground`), accents and status\n45:     (`--primary`/`--primary-foreground`, `--secondary`/`--secondary-foreground`,\n46:     `--accent`, `--link`, `--success`, `--warning`, `--error`), and chart series\n47:     (`--chart-1` through `--chart-6`). Keep `12px` (`text-xs`) as the minimum\n48:     text size unless the user asks otherwise.\n49: \n50: *   **Text, Surface & `:root` Rules**: Use semantic variables\n51:     (`bg-[var(--card)]`, `text-[var(--foreground)]`,\n52:     `text-[var(--muted-foreground)]`) instead of hardcoded dark/light utility\n53:     classes (e.g., `bg-slate-900`, `text-white`) for high contrast across both\n54:     light and dark themes. Never declare local color fallbacks on `:root` or\n55:     reference host-internal `var(--vscode-*)` or `var(--app-*)` tokens.\n56: \n57: *   **HTML Boilerplate Template**: Recommended flush inline template:\n58: \n59:     ```html\n60:     <!DOCTYPE html>"}
{"step_index":1223,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-17T03:35:05Z","input_tokens":3158,"cache_read_tokens":211006,"output_tokens":2037,"thinking":"The user wants to visualize atomic movement in a slab model using Winmostar, specifically the data from a 100-step simulation. The key is identifying the correct file path for Winmostar to load the animation data. Initial focus is on the NAS side Windows path where the data should reside.\n\n\nThe data file path on the NAS side is identified, along with the corresponding local WSL path. The file to load in Winmostar is likely `md_animation.xyz`, or potentially `md_nvt_animation.xyz`. The visualization process involves dragging and dropping the file into Winmostar or using the open file function, then using the MD animation controls to play the simulation.\n\n\nThe visualization reveals three key phases of atomic movement. Initially, surface silicon atoms rapidly sink inward, creating a buckled surface structure while carbon atoms remain on the outer layer. Next, the vacuum gap between the slabs slightly expands and contracts. Finally, the surface reconstruction energy propagates internally, leading to thermal vibrations throughout the slab, achieving a state of equilibrium.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/home/WORKSTATION4WSL/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/mdi_env_rtx5080/bin/python3 -c \"\nimport os, numpy as np\n\nunrel_dir = 'aimd_2H-SiC_10-10_8periods_vac3.0A_unrelaxed_300K_100steps_rtx5080'\nwith open(os.path.join(unrel_dir, 'dump.lammpstrj')) as f:\n    lines = f.readlines()\n\nframes = {}\ni = 0\nwhile i < len(lines):\n    if 'ITEM: TIMESTEP' in lines[i]:\n        step = int(lines[i+1].strip())\n        natoms = int(lines[i+3].strip())\n        data = []\n        for l in lines[i+9:i+9+natoms]:\n            parts = l.split()\n            data.append([int(parts[0]), int(parts[1]), float(parts[2]), float(parts[3]), float(parts[4])])\n        data.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n        frames[step] = np.array(data)\n        i += 9 + natoms\n    else:\n        i += 1\n\ninit = frames[0]\ntypes = init[:, 1].astype(int)\nz0 = init[:, 4]\n\n# Top surface (Layer 16): z0 > 22.0 (9 Si atoms, 9 C atoms)\ntop_mask = z0 > 22.0\ntop_si = top_mask & (types == 2)\ntop_c = top_mask & (types == 1)\n\n# Bottom surface (Layer 1): z0 < 3.5 (9 Si atoms, 9 C atoms)\nbot_mask = z0 < 3.5\nbot_si = bot_mask & (types == 2)\nbot_c = bot_mask & (types == 1)\n\nprint(f'{\\\"Step\\\":>4} | {\\\"Top_C_z\\\":>9} | {\\\"Top_Si_z\\\":>9} | {\\\"Top_Buckle(C-Si)\\\":>17} | {\\\"Bot_C_z\\\":>9} | {\\\"Bot_Si_z\\\":>9} | {\\\"Bot_Buckle(Si-C)\\\":>17} | {\\\"Gap(A)\\\":>8}')\nprint('-'*105)\nfor s in [0, 5, 10, 19, 27, 40, 50, 55, 70, 85, 100]:\n    arr = frames[s]\n    z = arr[:, 4]\n    tc = z[top_c].mean()\n    tsi = z[top_si].mean()\n    t_buckle = tc - tsi # In top surface, C is higher than Si (C points outward, Si sinks inward)\n    \n    bc = z[bot_c].mean()\n    bsi = z[bot_si].mean()\n    b_buckle = bsi - bc # In bottom surface, z decreases outward. Si sinks inward (+z), C stays outward (-z), so (Si_z - C_z) > 0\n    \n    gap = (z.min() + 25.5354013) - z.max()\n    print(f'{s:4d} | {tc:9.4f} | {tsi:9.4f} | {t_buckle:+17.4f} | {bc:9.4f} | {bsi:9.4f} | {b_buckle:+17.4f} | {gap:8.4f}')\n\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Analyze surface buckling dynamics across key steps"}}]}
